{"meta":{"chapter_title":"AI Ethics and Ethobot 3.2","title":"Ethobot Transcript Annotation","description":"CAT 531 textbook section connecting ethobot transcript annotation to Ethobot transcript annotation showing where ethical reasoning changed, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","course":"cat531-supplement","chapter":"04","section":"05","learning_objectives":[{"id":"cat531-supplement-04-05-lo-01","statement":"Analyze how the Ethobot transcript annotation showing where ethical reasoning changed shows a defensible decision in CAT 531"},{"id":"cat531-supplement-04-05-lo-02","statement":"Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision"},{"id":"cat531-supplement-04-05-lo-03","statement":"Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions"},{"id":"cat531-supplement-04-05-lo-04","statement":"Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible"}],"concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_ai_ethics_and_ethobot_3_2","cat531_supplement_ethobot_transcript_annotation","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"prereq_section_ids":["cat531-supplement/04/04"],"practice_ids":["cat531_supplement_4_5_ethobot_transcript_annotation_artifact_signal","cat531_supplement_4_5_ethobot_transcript_annotation_annotation_signal","cat531_supplement_4_5_ethobot_transcript_annotation_ai_boundary","cat531_supplement_4_5_ethobot_transcript_annotation_calibration"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":5,"top_tier_hardened":false,"lo_practice_map":{"cat531-supplement-04-05-lo-01":["cat531_supplement_4_5_ethobot_transcript_annotation_annotation_signal","cat531_supplement_4_5_ethobot_transcript_annotation_artifact_signal","cat531_supplement_4_5_ethobot_transcript_annotation_ai_boundary","cat531_supplement_4_5_ethobot_transcript_annotation_calibration"],"cat531-supplement-04-05-lo-02":["cat531_supplement_4_5_ethobot_transcript_annotation_annotation_signal"],"cat531-supplement-04-05-lo-03":["cat531_supplement_4_5_ethobot_transcript_annotation_annotation_signal"],"cat531-supplement-04-05-lo-04":["cat531_supplement_4_5_ethobot_transcript_annotation_annotation_signal"]},"alignment_method":"deterministic lexical mapping v2 with direct-evidence fallback; instructor review required"},"content":"# Ethobot Transcript Annotation\n\n<figure-block caption=\"Organizational reference model connecting Ethobot Transcript Annotation, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning to an evidence-based revision decision.\" conceptid=\"cat531_supplement_ai_ethics_and_ethobot_3_2\" purpose=\"organizational\" source=\"ALGET original instructional diagram\" license=\"Project-authored; repository license\">\n  <img src=\"/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat531-supplement/04-05-ethobot-transcript-annotation.png\" alt=\"Three connected concept cards for Ethobot Transcript Annotation: Ethobot Transcript Annotation, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning, followed by an evidence-to-revision transfer prompt.\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" />\n</figure-block>\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nSection 01 asked you to find where reasoning shifted. This section asks the harder, more transferable question: would another teacher, reading the same transcript with your method, mark the same place? That is the difference between a hunch and a method. Highlighting a turn in yellow and writing \"good thinking\" records your reaction; it cannot be checked, reproduced, or defended. A coding scheme, a small set of defined labels you apply consistently turn by turn, converts a private intuition into evidence two readers can agree on. This is the move that lets transcript annotation function as assessment rather than impression.\n\nThe reason this matters for a teacher specifically is fairness. If your annotation method is subjective, then the students whose phrasing happens to match your taste get credited with \"reasoning\" while others do not, and you would never know. A defined scheme with written warrants forces you to state, for each coded turn, the observable feature that earned the code, which is the same discipline that makes any rubric defensible to a parent, a colleague, or yourself a week later when the memory of the lesson has faded.\n\n**Recent evidence.** The bias mechanism is documented, not assumed: language models and the judgment systems built on them have been shown to penalize non-standard English dialects, including African American English, judging the same reasoning less favorably when its phrasing diverges from a \"standard\" (Fleisig et al., 2024; Hofmann et al., 2024). A defined, warrant-based scheme is the safeguard, because it ties credit to an observable reasoning move rather than to discourse style.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Define a small, mutually exclusive code scheme (for example, claim, reframing question, reasoning revision) with criteria, not vibes.\n- Apply the codes turn by turn and write a one-line warrant beside each high-value code.\n- Estimate inter-rater agreement by asking whether a colleague using your definitions would code the same turn the same way, and tighten definitions where they would not.\n- Distinguish reproducible coding from subjective highlighting, and explain why reproducibility is an equity safeguard.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nTwo teachers annotate the same Ethobot transcript of a high-school debate on AI and cheating. The first highlights three turns and writes \"strong reasoning.\" The second applies a three-code scheme and marks Turn 3 as a reasoning revision with the warrant \"now conditions cheating on teacher permission.\" A department head asks both to justify their marks. The first can only say \"it felt insightful.\" The second can hand over the scheme and the warrant and invite anyone to apply it and check. Only the second has produced something that survives scrutiny, and only the second has a method she can hand to a student teacher and trust them to use the same way.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nTranscript annotation becomes a method when it rests on a code scheme: a short list of labels with definitions clear enough that a second rater would assign them the same way. The aim is not elaborate taxonomy but reproducibility. Three design principles keep a scheme usable.\n\nFirst, keep the codes few and mutually exclusive. A scheme of three or four labels (a fixed claim, a question that reframes, a turn that revises a prior position) is applied consistently; a scheme of fifteen overlapping labels is applied differently every time, which destroys agreement.\n\nSecond, attach a warrant to every high-value code. The warrant is the one-line observable reason the turn earned the label: not \"good reasoning\" but \"revises the categorical claim by adding a condition.\" The warrant is what a second rater checks against, and what makes your call inspectable.\n\nThird, test for agreement and tighten where it fails. If you imagine a colleague applying your definitions and suspect they would code a turn differently, the definition is too loose. The fix is to sharpen the criterion until the disagreement disappears, which is the practical meaning of reliability. The simplest formal check is percent agreement (you and a colleague agree on N of M coded turns); formal studies report a chance-corrected statistic such as Cohen's kappa (Cohen, 1960) because two raters can agree by luck on a small code set. The imagine-a-colleague move is the practical floor for a single teacher; the named statistics are where the same idea goes when stakes rise.\n\nThe course anchors apply directly. **Teacher professional judgment** is exercised in defining the scheme and writing warrants; the platform cannot decide for you what counts as a reasoning revision. **Critical edtech studies** (Selwyn; see Pangrazio & Selwyn, 2023) warns that a scheme can smuggle in bias, rewarding the discourse style of dominant-culture students, so the criteria must describe reasoning moves, not eloquence. **[AI and education datafication](https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2020.1686017)** reminds you that an Ethobot transcript is data about minors; a reproducible scheme makes your use of that data accountable rather than arbitrary.\n\n## Worked Example: Building and Applying an Annotation Code Scheme\n\nThis section is about the annotation method itself: a small, consistent code scheme a teacher can apply turn by turn so two readers would agree. Work it as numbered steps on the excerpt below.\n\nTranscript excerpt (Ethobot 3.2, high-school AI-cheating debate):\n\n1. Student: \"Using AI to write is always cheating.\"\n2. Ethobot: \"Does it matter whether the teacher allowed it?\"\n3. Student: \"If the teacher allowed it then it is not cheating, so the rule depends on the assignment.\"\n4. Student: \"But what about a student who cannot afford a tutor and uses AI instead?\"\n\nStep-by-step coding:\n\n- Step 1. Define a 3-code scheme. CL = fixed claim, QU = a question that reframes, RE = reasoning that revises the prior position.\n- Step 2. Apply codes per turn. Turn 1 = CL. Turn 2 = QU. Turn 3 = RE (revises the categorical claim). Turn 4 = RE (introduces an equity dimension).\n- Step 3. Write the warrant beside each RE code. Turn 3 warrant = \"now conditions cheating on teacher permission.\" Turn 4 warrant = \"now weighs access and fairness.\"\n- Step 4. Check inter-rater agreement. Ask whether a colleague applying the same scheme would code Turn 3 as RE. If the codes are subjective, tighten the definitions.\n\nBefore (weak): highlighting Turns 3 and 4 in yellow with the note \"good thinking here.\"\n\nAfter (strong): \"Codes: T1 CL, T2 QU, T3 RE (warrant: cheating now conditioned on permission), T4 RE (warrant: equity of access introduced). Scheme is the CL/QU/RE rubric above so another rater can reproduce the coding. The QU at T2 is what triggered both RE turns.\"\n\nThe strong annotation uses a reusable code scheme with warrants, not subjective highlighting, so the judgment is reproducible.\n\nMatch each code in the scheme to the observable feature that earns it. A scheme is only reproducible when the definitions are this precise.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"match\" items='[{\"term\":\"CL (fixed claim)\", \"definition\":\"A stated position with no weighing of competing considerations, e.g. \\\"Using AI to write is always cheating.\\\"\"}, {\"term\":\"QU (reframing question)\", \"definition\":\"A prompt that opens a new lens or condition, e.g. \\\"Does it matter whether the teacher allowed it?\\\"\"}, {\"term\":\"RE (reasoning revision)\", \"definition\":\"A turn that revises a prior position by adding a condition or dimension, e.g. \\\"If the teacher allowed it then it is not cheating.\\\"\"}, {\"term\":\"Warrant\", \"definition\":\"The one-line observable reason a turn earned its code, checkable by a second rater, e.g. \\\"now conditions cheating on permission.\\\"\"}]'></sequence-builder>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nBefore an annotation counts as evidence, it must pass three checks:\n\n1. **Defined scheme:** Are the codes few, mutually exclusive, and defined by observable criteria?\n2. **Warranted codes:** Does each high-value code carry a one-line reason a second reader could verify?\n3. **Agreement:** Would a colleague using your definitions code the contested turns the same way?\n\nIf a code rests on \"it felt like reasoning,\" sharpen the criterion until the feeling becomes a feature.\n\n<inline-check question=\"Two raters use your scheme. Turn 4 reads: 'But what about a student who cannot afford a tutor and uses AI instead?' Your warrant says 'sounded thoughtful.' Why will this warrant fail the agreement check?\" explanation=\"'Sounded thoughtful' describes how the turn sounded, not what it did. A second rater cannot confirm a tone judgment. A reproducible warrant points to an observable feature, such as 'introduces an equity-of-access dimension,' which a colleague can verify against the line.\" options='[{\"text\":\"It is fine; tone is an observable feature\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"It describes tone, not an observable move a second rater can confirm\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"It is too long to be a one-line warrant\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"It uses the wrong code letter\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\nA department head now asks both you and a colleague to defend a contested code. The defensibility of your warrant is what is on trial.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"Defending a contested code to a department head\",\"start\":\"n1\",\"nodes\":{\"n1\":{\"prompt\":\"You coded Turn 3 (\\\"If the teacher allowed it then it is not cheating, so the rule depends on the assignment\\\") as RE. The department head asks: why RE and not just a restated claim? What do you offer?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"\\\"It felt like a deeper level of thinking\\\"\",\"next\":\"n2\",\"feedback\":\"A feeling is not a criterion. A second rater cannot check it, so the code is not yet reproducible.\"},{\"label\":\"\\\"The warrant: it conditions cheating on teacher permission, adding a condition that was not in Turn 1\\\"\",\"next\":\"n3\",\"feedback\":\"This is an observable feature, the addition of a condition, that a second rater can confirm against the line.\"},{\"label\":\"\\\"I highlighted it in yellow because it stood out\\\"\",\"next\":\"n4\",\"feedback\":\"Highlighting marks where you looked, not why. It carries no criterion anyone can check.\"}]},\"n2\":{\"prompt\":\"Convert the feeling into a checkable feature.\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Point to the added condition: cheating now depends on permission\",\"next\":\"n3\",\"feedback\":\"Now the code rests on an observable move.\"}],\"feedback\":\"\"},\"n3\":{\"prompt\":\"The head asks a colleague to apply your scheme blind. They also code Turn 3 as RE. What has this established?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Inter-rater agreement: the criterion is reproducible\",\"next\":\"n5\",\"feedback\":\"Right. Agreement on a contested turn is the practical meaning of reliability.\"},{\"label\":\"Nothing; one colleague is not proof\",\"next\":\"n5\",\"feedback\":\"It is evidence, not proof, but reproducible agreement on the contested turn is exactly what the scheme is built to produce.\"}]},\"n4\":{\"prompt\":\"Replace the highlight with a coded warrant.\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"State the observable move the turn made\",\"next\":\"n3\",\"feedback\":\"Coding records why; highlighting only records where.\"}],\"feedback\":\"\"},\"n5\":{\"prompt\":\"The head asks why you did not build a richer twelve-code scheme to capture more nuance.\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"\\\"Three or four well-defined codes are applied consistently; twelve fuzzy ones destroy the agreement the scheme exists to create\\\"\",\"next\":\"end\",\"feedback\":\"Correct. Reproducibility, not taxonomy, is the goal.\"},{\"label\":\"\\\"A twelve-code scheme would be more impressive\\\"\",\"next\":\"n6\",\"feedback\":\"Impressiveness is not the standard; consistency is.\"}]},\"n6\":{\"prompt\":\"Re-anchor on the standard.\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Few mutually exclusive codes beat many fuzzy ones for agreement\",\"next\":\"end\",\"feedback\":\"Right.\"}],\"feedback\":\"\"},\"end\":{\"prompt\":\"You defended the code with an observable warrant and a reproducible scheme.\",\"feedback\":\"A code counts as evidence when its warrant names a feature a second rater can confirm, and the scheme is small enough to apply consistently.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe first misreading is mistaking highlighting for coding: color marks where you looked, not why, and carries no criterion anyone can check. The second is over-engineering the scheme. A teacher who builds a twelve-code taxonomy will apply it inconsistently and lose the very agreement the scheme was meant to create; three or four well-defined codes beat twelve fuzzy ones every time.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nApply the method to a middle-school transcript on whether classmates should report a friend who plagiarized. Define the scheme: CL (fixed stance), QU (reframing question), RE (revision of prior stance). Code the turns: a student opens \"You always report cheating\" (CL); Ethobot asks \"What do you owe the friend, and what do you owe the class?\" (QU); the student says \"Maybe I talk to the friend first, then report only if it keeps happening\" (RE, warrant: \"introduces a graded loyalty-versus-fairness response\"). Now run the agreement check: would a colleague also code that last turn RE? Yes, because the warrant points to an observable feature (the addition of a condition), not to tone. Had the warrant read \"sounded thoughtful,\" a second rater could not confirm it, and the code would fail. The same three-code scheme that handled the cheating debate handles this with no modification, which is the point of building a method rather than reacting to each transcript fresh.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nKeep a two-column note: the code on the left, the verbatim warrant on the right, and flag any code whose warrant you could not defend to a skeptical colleague. A strong reader of a transcript can hand over the scheme and predict, with confidence, how someone else would code the same turns. If your warrants describe how a turn sounded rather than what it did, the scheme is not yet reliable.\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nA strong annotation includes the code scheme with definitions, codes every turn, warrants the high-value codes with observable reasons, and notes where agreement is shaky. Formatting is the last layer. If a code has no checkable warrant, mark the gap and ask TeachGen@i to compare your definition against a tighter one rather than for a general explanation of coding.\n\nA useful closing line for the artifact sounds like this: \"I coded Turn 3 as a reasoning revision with the warrant 'conditions cheating on teacher permission,' an observable feature a second rater can confirm; I rejected color-highlighting because it would have recorded where I looked without a criterion anyone could reproduce.\"\n\n<self-explain prompt=\"The section claims reproducibility is an equity safeguard, not just a methodological nicety. Explain why a subjective annotation method can quietly produce unfair credit.\" answer=\"If a code rests on taste rather than an observable criterion, then a teacher tends to credit 'reasoning' to the turns whose phrasing matches their own discourse style, which usually favors dominant-culture students. The students whose reasoning arrives in less familiar phrasing get no credit, and because the method is subjective the teacher never sees the pattern and cannot correct it. A defined scheme with written warrants forces the teacher to name, for each coded turn, the observable reasoning move that earned it, so a quiet bias toward eloquence is replaced by a criterion any rater can check, which is what makes the credit fair.\"></self-explain>\n\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the scheme from an ethics debate to a math-reasoning transcript, where the \"reasoning revision\" you are looking for is a student abandoning a wrong procedure after a probing question. The codes' names hold but their criteria must be rewritten for the new domain: a revision in math looks like a corrected step, not a reframed value. Working this version tests whether you grasp the underlying idea (define, warrant, check agreement) or only memorized the CL/QU/RE labels from the ethics case.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the Ethobot transcript annotation showing where ethical reasoning changed packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat531-supplement/04-05-ethobot-transcript-annotation-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"Ethobot transcript annotation showing where ethical reasoning changed\" course=\"CAT 531\" section=\"04.05\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the Ethobot transcript annotation showing where ethical reasoning changed.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the Ethobot transcript annotation showing where ethical reasoning changed for CAT 531 04.05. A peer comment points to an adaptive move issue connected to critical edtech studies. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Concept Map\n\nThese are the concepts this section connects. Trace how a reproducible code scheme turns annotation into evidence-based revision.\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat531_supplement_ai_ethics_and_ethobot_3_2\", \"cat531_supplement_ethobot_transcript_annotation\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which clue should guide what support or revision comes next?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The combination of the note, artifact evidence, confidence, and revision quality.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"1\" conceptid=\"cat531_supplement_ethobot_transcript_annotation\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, make sure your trace records evidence source, risk, support choice, and revision quality. That is what separates professional judgment from simple completion.\n\n## References\n\n- [TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge). https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9620.2006.00684.x\n- Selwyn, N. (2016). critical edtech studies. https://doi.org/10.4324/9781315886350\n- Pangrazio, L., & Selwyn, N. (2023). Critical data literacies: Rethinking data and everyday life. MIT Press. https://doi.org/10.7551/mitpress/14155.001.0001\n- [AI and education datafication](https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2020.1686017)\n- Williamson, B., et al. (2023). Re-examining AI, automation and datafication in education. *Learning, Media and Technology, 48*(1). https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2023.2167830\n- teacher professional judgment. https://doi.org/10.1177/002205741319300304\n- Fleisig, E., et al. (2024). Linguistic bias in ChatGPT: Language models reinforce dialect discrimination. *Proceedings of EMNLP 2024*. https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2024.emnlp-main.750\n- Hofmann, V., et al. (2024). Dialect prejudice predicts AI decisions about people's character, employability, and criminality. https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2403.00742\n- Ning, Y., et al. (2024). Teachers' AI-TPACK: Exploring the relationship between knowledge elements. *Sustainability, 16*(3), 978. https://doi.org/10.3390/su16030978\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat531_supplement_4_5_ethobot_transcript_annotation_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_ethobot_transcript_annotation","stem":"A teacher annotates a transcript by highlighting two turns in yellow and writing 'good thinking here.' What is the core methodological weakness?","options":["Yellow is hard to read on screen.","There is no defined code scheme, so another rater could not reproduce which turns count as reasoning.","The note is too short to earn full marks.","The teacher highlighted two turns instead of one."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Annotation is a method; without a reproducible code scheme the judgment is just subjective highlighting.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_4_5_highlighting_is_coding","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_ethobot_transcript_annotation","evidence_based_revision"],"learning_objective_ids":["cat531-supplement-04-05-lo-01"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_4_5_ethobot_transcript_annotation_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_ethobot_transcript_annotation","stem":"A turn is coded RE (reasoning). What must accompany that code to make the annotation defensible?","options":["The time the turn was spoken.","A warrant naming the specific phrase or move in the turn that justifies the reasoning label.","The student's name and grade.","A count of how many words the student used."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A code without a warrant cannot be inspected; the warrant ties the label to evidence in the turn.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_4_5_no_warrant","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_ethobot_transcript_annotation","evidence_based_revision"],"learning_objective_ids":["cat531-supplement-04-05-lo-01","cat531-supplement-04-05-lo-02","cat531-supplement-04-05-lo-03","cat531-supplement-04-05-lo-04"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_4_5_ethobot_transcript_annotation_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_ethobot_transcript_annotation","stem":"Before reporting an annotation as evidence, which check tells you the code scheme is sound rather than personal?","options":["Whether the colors look professional.","Whether a colleague applying the same code definitions would code the same turns the same way.","Whether the transcript is at least ten turns long.","Whether the AI generated the transcript quickly."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Inter-rater agreement is the test of a reproducible annotation method.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_4_5_ignoring_reliability","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_ethobot_transcript_annotation","ai_supported_learning"],"learning_objective_ids":["cat531-supplement-04-05-lo-01"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_4_5_ethobot_transcript_annotation_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_evidence_based_revision","stem":"Using the CL/QU/RE scheme, a student says 'If the teacher allowed it, it is not cheating, so the rule depends on the assignment.' Which coding and warrant is most defensible?","options":["CL, because the student stated an opinion.","RE, with the warrant that the student now conditions cheating on teacher permission, revising the earlier categorical claim.","QU, because the sentence contains the word if.","No code, because the turn is too long to classify."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The turn revises the prior fixed claim by adding a condition, which is reasoning (RE) with a clear warrant.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_4_5_no_warrant","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_evidence_based_revision","cat531_supplement_ethobot_transcript_annotation"],"learning_objective_ids":["cat531-supplement-04-05-lo-01"]}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"cat531_supplement_4_5_highlighting_is_coding","pattern":"subjective_highlighting_mistaken_for_coding","description":"Treating highlighting interesting lines as transcript annotation, with no defined code scheme another rater could reproduce","trigger":"learner marks lines as 'good thinking' without a defined code or warrant","feedback":"Define a small code scheme (for example claim, question, reasoning) and write a warrant for each code so another rater could reproduce your annotation.","rail_action":"represent"},{"id":"cat531_supplement_4_5_no_warrant","pattern":"code_applied_without_warrant","description":"Tagging a turn with a code but not stating the evidence in the turn that justifies the code","trigger":"learner labels a turn 'reasoning' but does not quote or paraphrase what changed","feedback":"Beside each code, write the warrant: the specific phrase or move in that turn that justifies the label.","rail_action":"explain"},{"id":"cat531_supplement_4_5_ignoring_reliability","pattern":"ignoring_inter_rater_reliability","description":"Assuming a personal annotation is valid without checking whether another teacher would code the same turns the same way","trigger":"learner never asks whether the codes are reproducible by a colleague","feedback":"Check inter-rater agreement. If a colleague using your scheme would code differently, tighten the code definitions.","rail_action":"ask"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"aa41d7583da4302f03a2bdd53673f94196bca1d651039b92b39434eb7dda93ef","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}